WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.625 --> 00:00:02.544
(host)
Of all the many terrifying
fae-like monsters

00:00:02.627 --> 00:00:04.045
haunting the waters
of the world,

00:00:04.129 --> 00:00:05.547
the Nixie stands out.

00:00:05.630 --> 00:00:07.340
These half-human
half-fish beings

00:00:07.424 --> 00:00:09.634
may at first glance
look familiar,

00:00:09.718 --> 00:00:12.137
but they are much
older and much scarier

00:00:12.220 --> 00:00:14.597
than the usual mermaid
of pop culture.

00:00:14.681 --> 00:00:15.974
Thanks, Ariel.

00:00:16.057 --> 00:00:18.977
They love to shapeshift and
sing mesmerizing melodies

00:00:19.060 --> 00:00:22.313
all while harboring
an insatiable desire for humans

00:00:22.397 --> 00:00:25.567
which often veers
into a dark obsession.

00:00:25.650 --> 00:00:27.360
So what is a Nixie?

00:00:27.444 --> 00:00:30.238
A water sprite,
a nymph, a mermaid?

00:00:30.321 --> 00:00:31.573
Technically, yeah.

00:00:31.656 --> 00:00:33.491
[adventurous music]

00:00:33.575 --> 00:00:37.495
I'm Dr. Emily Zarka
and this is "Monstrum."

00:00:37.579 --> 00:00:39.873
The Nixie belongs
to a large Germanic tradition

00:00:39.956 --> 00:00:42.333
of murderous
shapeshifting water nymphs

00:00:42.417 --> 00:00:43.710
that also appear in folklore

00:00:43.793 --> 00:00:46.129
from Switzerland
and Scandinavia.

00:00:46.212 --> 00:00:48.173
Known throughout
folklore by many names,

00:00:48.256 --> 00:00:50.508
these deadly and
enchanting water creatures

00:00:50.592 --> 00:00:52.385
may take the appearance
of an old human

00:00:52.469 --> 00:00:55.013
with green teeth,
green skin, and green hair.

00:00:55.096 --> 00:00:56.639
Most of the time, though,

00:00:56.723 --> 00:00:58.725
they take the form
of a beautiful human

00:00:58.808 --> 00:01:01.728
covered with moss and seaweed
and decorated with shells.

00:01:01.811 --> 00:01:03.396
Some German legends
describe the Nixie

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:05.440
with slit ears or fishtails

00:01:05.523 --> 00:01:06.816
when they're in the water.

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:09.694
They have the ability to
appear clothed on land

00:01:09.778 --> 00:01:11.654
but the water dripping
from their garments

00:01:11.738 --> 00:01:13.740
gives away their true identity.

00:01:13.823 --> 00:01:16.326
Throwing stones into the water
can irritate the Nixie

00:01:16.409 --> 00:01:18.411
who may create
a storm in response.

00:01:18.495 --> 00:01:21.164
Yep. These water monsters
control the weather.

00:01:21.247 --> 00:01:23.208
An additionally scary thing
about the Nixie

00:01:23.291 --> 00:01:25.794
is that they can haunt
any body of water.

00:01:25.877 --> 00:01:29.756
No well, stream, river,
pond, lake or ocean is safe.

00:01:29.839 --> 00:01:32.467
They lure their victims
with music, usually singing,

00:01:32.550 --> 00:01:34.219
or in some stories they imitate

00:01:34.302 --> 00:01:36.471
a crying child to
draw someone near

00:01:36.554 --> 00:01:39.724
before seizing and pulling
their prey below the surface.

00:01:39.808 --> 00:01:41.601
What they do with them
from there varies.

00:01:41.684 --> 00:01:44.395
In many tales, the Nixie serve

00:01:44.479 --> 00:01:46.564
as a cautionary
tale for children

00:01:46.648 --> 00:01:49.484
who are told to avoid the water
so as not to be grabbed,

00:01:49.567 --> 00:01:51.277
implying that
they will not return.

00:01:51.361 --> 00:01:52.445
In other stories,

00:01:52.529 --> 00:01:55.114
the monsters require
an annual human sacrifice

00:01:55.198 --> 00:01:56.491
and they prefer children.

00:01:56.574 --> 00:01:59.410
Stories of the deadly Nixie
usually end with death

00:01:59.494 --> 00:02:02.789
but there are also accounts
of a more romantic persuasion.

00:02:02.872 --> 00:02:04.833
Sometimes they will
transform into horses

00:02:04.916 --> 00:02:06.459
to carry their
willing human lovers

00:02:06.543 --> 00:02:08.461
to their crystal palaces.

00:02:08.545 --> 00:02:09.671
Some of these monsters

00:02:09.754 --> 00:02:11.840
apparently have alluring
underwater homes.

00:02:11.923 --> 00:02:13.758
So yes, these stories implied

00:02:13.842 --> 00:02:15.802
that the Nixie mate with humans.

00:02:15.885 --> 00:02:17.512
Though such romantic encounters

00:02:17.595 --> 00:02:19.472
are not encouraged
in their society,

00:02:19.556 --> 00:02:22.934
said to be ruled by a malevolent
water diety akin to Satan.

00:02:23.017 --> 00:02:25.520
Named Nick
from the ancient Icelandic,

00:02:25.603 --> 00:02:27.313
he will kill any of the Nixie

00:02:27.397 --> 00:02:29.566
who dare to even
talk to a human.

00:02:29.649 --> 00:02:32.735
Described as more of
a classic mermaid or merman,

00:02:32.819 --> 00:02:36.489
Old Nick is the fishtailed
father of all the Nixies.

00:02:36.573 --> 00:02:38.032
Over time, and in some regions,

00:02:38.116 --> 00:02:39.868
this water deity
became watered down

00:02:39.951 --> 00:02:42.245
into the Nixies who
borrow his name.

00:02:42.328 --> 00:02:44.539
Because of this
etymological connection,

00:02:44.622 --> 00:02:46.416
the Nixie is
associated with malice

00:02:46.499 --> 00:02:49.294
more often than many of
their global counterparts.

00:02:49.377 --> 00:02:51.629
Before Nick,
and therefore all Nixies,

00:02:51.713 --> 00:02:52.839
somewhere around 700 CE,

00:02:52.922 --> 00:02:56.676
the famous text Beowulf
mentioned nickers,

00:02:56.759 --> 00:02:58.761
violent water monsters.

00:02:58.845 --> 00:03:01.973
Some folklore theorized that
Nixies may have originated

00:03:02.056 --> 00:03:03.808
from ancient spiritual practices

00:03:03.892 --> 00:03:05.852
of river and water god worship

00:03:05.935 --> 00:03:09.063
that turned nefarious after the
introduction of Christianity

00:03:09.147 --> 00:03:11.190
when water worship
was discouraged.

00:03:11.274 --> 00:03:14.569
Still, whispers and legends
of the monster persisted

00:03:14.652 --> 00:03:16.571
and became popular
across the globe

00:03:16.654 --> 00:03:19.282
thanks to a few key books
in the 19th century.

00:03:19.365 --> 00:03:22.744
In the German fairy tale
"Undine" published in 1811,

00:03:22.827 --> 00:03:25.204
a Nixie, here
called water sprite,

00:03:25.288 --> 00:03:26.998
appears on the doorstep
of a couple

00:03:27.081 --> 00:03:28.791
whose daughter drowned
earlier that day.

00:03:28.875 --> 00:03:32.545
Appearing as a young
human girl dripping wet,

00:03:32.629 --> 00:03:35.048
she is taken in by
the grieving parents.

00:03:35.131 --> 00:03:38.051
She's exquisitely
beautiful with golden hair,

00:03:38.134 --> 00:03:40.637
but is also known to
have supernatural powers

00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:42.472
like controlling
water and the weather,

00:03:42.555 --> 00:03:43.973
and a fiery temperament.

00:03:44.057 --> 00:03:45.934
Eventually,
Undine marries a knight,

00:03:46.017 --> 00:03:49.938
and overnight becomes a nicer,
more accommodating person.

00:03:50.021 --> 00:03:54.025
She claims the change is due
to her now possessing a soul.

00:03:54.108 --> 00:03:56.569
Apparently, as a water
sprite she didn't have one

00:03:56.653 --> 00:03:59.489
and can only get one
by marrying a human.

00:03:59.572 --> 00:04:00.740
Even after she reveals
her true nature,

00:04:00.823 --> 00:04:03.493
including her
people's great beauty

00:04:03.576 --> 00:04:05.536
and otherworldly singing voices,

00:04:05.620 --> 00:04:07.538
her husband seems okay
with the whole thing,

00:04:07.622 --> 00:04:10.708
which granted doesn't seem
all that bad considering.

00:04:10.792 --> 00:04:12.418
Her water sprite uncle, however,

00:04:12.502 --> 00:04:15.380
is not pleased with this
intervening marriage

00:04:15.463 --> 00:04:17.882
and repeatedly attacks the
couple and their company

00:04:17.966 --> 00:04:21.511
with waves, frightening
apparitions, storms, and floods.

00:04:21.594 --> 00:04:23.596
Eventually, Undine
is forced to return

00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:25.974
to the Nixie's underwater
crystal kingdom

00:04:26.057 --> 00:04:29.352
after her husband accuses
her of being a sorceress.

00:04:29.435 --> 00:04:31.145
Meanwhile, her
former knight husband

00:04:31.229 --> 00:04:32.772
remarries his former love

00:04:32.855 --> 00:04:35.316
and the old couple's
biological daughter,

00:04:35.400 --> 00:04:38.569
who in a strange twist,
didn't drown after all.

00:04:38.653 --> 00:04:40.530
Due to the rules of
the Nixie society,

00:04:40.613 --> 00:04:42.323
Undine must reappear on land

00:04:42.407 --> 00:04:45.410
and give her husband a killer
kiss for his infidelity,

00:04:45.493 --> 00:04:47.120
drowning him with her tears.

00:04:47.203 --> 00:04:49.163
So we have a beautiful,
kind Nixie

00:04:49.247 --> 00:04:51.666
who, yes, is forced
to kill her husband

00:04:51.749 --> 00:04:54.460
but the tale is presented
as a tragic love story.

00:04:54.544 --> 00:04:57.046
Oh, and this story,
yeah, it helped inspire

00:04:57.130 --> 00:04:59.549
Han's Christian Anderson's
"The Little Mermaid."

00:04:59.632 --> 00:05:02.093
The Nixie in the
Brothers Grimm 1857 tale,

00:05:02.176 --> 00:05:05.722
"Nix of the Mill-Pond" lacks
any redeeming qualities.

00:05:05.805 --> 00:05:07.640
A man who recently
lost his fortune

00:05:07.724 --> 00:05:10.101
sees a beautiful woman
rise from the waters

00:05:10.184 --> 00:05:11.227
of the river by his mill.

00:05:11.310 --> 00:05:14.188
He recognizes her as
a Nixie straight off

00:05:14.272 --> 00:05:15.606
and even though
he is frightened,

00:05:15.690 --> 00:05:17.734
he accepts a strange
bargain from her.

00:05:17.817 --> 00:05:20.737
She promises to
restore his wealth

00:05:20.820 --> 00:05:24.157
if he pays her, "The young
thing which has just been born."

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:25.783
The young thing turns out
to be his firstborn son

00:05:25.867 --> 00:05:29.412
whom his wife has
only just delivered.

00:05:29.495 --> 00:05:31.539
When he realizes the
horror of what he has done,

00:05:31.622 --> 00:05:34.125
he raises the boy
to fear the water.

00:05:34.208 --> 00:05:37.420
This works until his son is
now a married man himself.

00:05:37.503 --> 00:05:39.589
One day after a hunt,
without thinking,

00:05:39.672 --> 00:05:42.133
the son washes his
hands in a pond.

00:05:42.216 --> 00:05:44.469
The Nixie seizes
the opportunity.

00:05:44.552 --> 00:05:46.596
She slowly rises
up from the water

00:05:46.679 --> 00:05:49.515
and with a big smile,
wraps her arms around him,

00:05:49.599 --> 00:05:51.809
dragging him down into
the water with her.

00:05:51.893 --> 00:05:54.395
His distraught wife goes
to the local witch for help

00:05:54.479 --> 00:05:57.607
who advises a series of
steps to bring him out.

00:05:57.690 --> 00:06:00.943
First, she combs her hair near
the water where he was taken.

00:06:01.027 --> 00:06:02.779
It works, sort of.

00:06:02.862 --> 00:06:06.115
Her husband's head momentarily
appears above the surface

00:06:06.199 --> 00:06:08.868
but quickly disappears
under the water again.

00:06:08.951 --> 00:06:10.745
The next day, she plays a flute,

00:06:10.828 --> 00:06:13.831
and this time half
of his body emerges.

00:06:13.915 --> 00:06:15.333
On the third day,

00:06:15.416 --> 00:06:17.126
she works a golden
spinning wheel by the pond

00:06:17.210 --> 00:06:19.378
and her husband fully emerges.

00:06:19.462 --> 00:06:21.047
He begins to leave the water

00:06:21.130 --> 00:06:23.841
but not before a tidal wave
suddenly rises up

00:06:23.925 --> 00:06:25.593
and starts heading towards them.

00:06:25.676 --> 00:06:29.013
The woman calls upon the witch
with one last plea for help.

00:06:29.097 --> 00:06:31.974
And help she does, by
turning the wife into a toad

00:06:32.058 --> 00:06:33.059
and the huntsman into a frog,

00:06:33.142 --> 00:06:37.313
enabling them to survive
the torrent of water.

00:06:37.396 --> 00:06:39.649
Luckily, they revert back
to their human forms,

00:06:39.732 --> 00:06:41.859
but unluckily, they are
separated by the wave.

00:06:41.943 --> 00:06:44.695
They live separate
lives for years

00:06:44.779 --> 00:06:48.282
until one day as fate would
have it, they cross paths.

00:06:48.366 --> 00:06:49.909
They do not recognize
one another,

00:06:49.992 --> 00:06:51.244
but become good friends.

00:06:51.327 --> 00:06:53.704
One day, the Huntsman plays
an interesting flute song,

00:06:53.788 --> 00:06:55.623
the very same one his wife did

00:06:55.706 --> 00:06:57.917
besides the pond so
many years before.

00:06:58.000 --> 00:06:59.794
They suddenly realize
who each other is

00:06:59.877 --> 00:07:01.337
and fall back in love.

00:07:01.420 --> 00:07:02.880
These stories
established the Nixie

00:07:02.964 --> 00:07:04.841
as both villains and victims

00:07:04.924 --> 00:07:07.802
and popularized the female
Nixie over the male Nix.

00:07:07.885 --> 00:07:10.721
Nixies, along with other
water nymphs and mermaids,

00:07:10.805 --> 00:07:12.974
appear often in
19th century fiction

00:07:13.057 --> 00:07:14.976
as folkloric femme fatales

00:07:15.059 --> 00:07:17.103
that reflect patriarchal
society's fear

00:07:17.186 --> 00:07:19.021
of the
increasingly-liberated woman

00:07:19.105 --> 00:07:20.606
and changing gender roles.

00:07:20.690 --> 00:07:23.276
Because literature emphasizes
the beauty of the Nixie,

00:07:23.359 --> 00:07:25.695
they're often seen as
projections of desire,

00:07:25.778 --> 00:07:27.488
largely through the male gaze,

00:07:27.572 --> 00:07:29.407
but others interpret
them as feminist symbols

00:07:29.490 --> 00:07:32.743
of the power of women and
nature, as allegories for death,

00:07:32.827 --> 00:07:35.705
or even as a reminder of
the dangers of drowning.

00:07:35.788 --> 00:07:38.207
I think more broadly,
these 19th-century Nixies

00:07:38.291 --> 00:07:39.834
gesture towards modernity,

00:07:39.917 --> 00:07:42.044
their tempting songs
and offers of treasure

00:07:42.128 --> 00:07:44.839
akin to the siren pull
of industrialization

00:07:44.922 --> 00:07:46.799
which, likewise, promises
luxury and wealth

00:07:46.883 --> 00:07:48.301
but at a severe cost.

00:07:48.384 --> 00:07:49.927
According to folklore,

00:07:50.011 --> 00:07:51.929
the Nixie lacks
maternal instinct,

00:07:52.013 --> 00:07:53.472
sensibility, and a soul.

00:07:53.556 --> 00:07:55.433
Having a fishtail,
supernatural foresight,

00:07:55.516 --> 00:07:57.143
or the ability to shapeshift

00:07:57.226 --> 00:08:00.396
are at odds with the parts of
them that are more human-like.

00:08:00.479 --> 00:08:03.733
But it's the human side that
presents the most danger.

00:08:03.816 --> 00:08:05.401
The mouth that can
tell the future

00:08:05.484 --> 00:08:06.736
or lure one to a watery death.

00:08:06.819 --> 00:08:10.281
Or the arms that pull
their prey down below.

00:08:10.364 --> 00:08:11.574
It's the human qualities

00:08:11.657 --> 00:08:14.702
that allow them to slip
into society unnoticed.

00:08:14.785 --> 00:08:16.287
A wolf among sheep,

00:08:16.370 --> 00:08:18.748
or rather,
a shark among seals.

00:08:21.500 --> 00:08:22.668
Old Nick is the--

00:08:22.752 --> 00:08:25.254
[whines] It went on my lip.
I'm sorry.

00:08:25.338 --> 00:08:26.172
[crew laughs]

00:08:26.255 --> 00:08:27.673
Okay. That time it landed on me.

